---
import MotionPage from '@/components/MotionPage.astro';
import MotionStage from '@/components/motion/MotionStage.astro';
import MotionUsage from '@/components/motion/MotionUsage.astro';
import MotionOptions from '@/components/motion/MotionOptions.astro';
import MotionTokens from '@/components/motion/MotionTokens.astro';
import Presence from '@/components/motion/scenes/Presence.astro';
import { PresenceControls } from '@/components/motion/scenes/PresenceControls';
import { MotionPresenceReact } from '@/examples/react/motion-presence.tsx';
import { MotionPresenceAngular } from '@/examples/angular/motion-presence.ts';
import MotionPresenceSvelte from '@/examples/svelte/motion-presence.svelte';
import { chipClass } from '@/components/motionDemo';
---
<MotionPage
  slug="presence"
  title="Motion: show and hide"
  description="An element that enters when it is inserted or shown, and leaves when it is hidden or removed, in HTML, React, Angular and Svelte."
>
  <MotionStage scene="presence" label="Live example: Show and hide" height={240}>
    <Fragment slot="controls">
      <PresenceControls client:visible toggleId="presence-page-toggle" cardId="presence-page-card" />
    </Fragment>
    <Presence id="presence-page-card" toggleId="presence-page-toggle" />
    <Fragment slot="readout"><span class={chipClass} data-presence-readout>data-motion="slide"</span></Fragment>
  </MotionStage>

  <h2>How to use it</h2>
  <p>
    Name the motion in <code>data-motion</code>, and set <code>hidden</code> to leave: the exit plays, and then the
    element is gone. Remove <code>hidden</code> to come back: the entry plays. The preset <code>slide</code> enters and
    leaves by rising 24 px while it fades.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>An element inserted into the page plays its entry, unless it is inserted with <code>hidden</code>.</li>
    <li>An element that is on the page when the runtime connects plays no entry.</li>
    <li>While the exit plays the element is <code>inert</code>; the runtime writes <code>data-presence</code> on it: <code>entering</code>, <code>present</code> or <code>exiting</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Focus</h2>
  <p>
    An exit sets <code>inert</code>, and the browser then drops the focus that was inside the element. When the exit
    starts with focus inside, name where the focus goes (WCAG 2.4.3). The scene's close button does it with
    <code>data-motion-restore-focus</code>:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`<div data-motion="fade" data-motion-restore-focus="#open-button">…</div>`}</code></pre>

  <h2>Use it in the frameworks</h2>
  <p>The live examples render a card with the <code>slide</code> preset. Each framework keeps the card until its exit has played.</p>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-presence" previewHeight={264}>
    <MotionPresenceReact slot="react" client:visible />
    <MotionPresenceAngular slot="angular" client:visible />
    <MotionPresenceSvelte slot="svelte" client:visible />
  </MotionUsage>
  <p>
    React: <code>&lt;Presence&gt;</code> around <code>&#123;open &amp;&amp; &lt;Motion name="slide"&gt;&#125;</code>, so the card stays rendered until its exit has played.
    Angular: add <code>Motion</code> to <code>imports</code>, and let <code>&#64;if</code> hold the element.
    Svelte: <code>&#123;&#64;attach motion('slide')&#125; transition:presence</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    In JavaScript, <code>motion(card, 'slide', &#123; settled: true &#125;)</code> binds a present element without its
    entry, and <code>setPresent(false)</code> runs the exit: it resolves to <code>'finished'</code> or
    <code>'cancelled'</code>, and never rejects.
  </p>

  <h2>Options</h2>
  <p>An appear and a disappear take the same options. The presence of an element is set by <code>hidden</code>, <code>present</code> or <code>setPresent</code>.</p>
  <MotionOptions verb="appear" />

  <h2>Timing and tokens</h2>
  <p>Without a timing, a presence uses the default springs: spatial for the movement, effects for the opacity.</p>
  <MotionTokens tokens={['spring.default.spatial', 'spring.default.effects']} />

  <h2>Reduced motion</h2>
  <p>
    Under reduced motion (<code>prefers-reduced-motion: reduce</code>, or <code>data-motion-policy="reduce"</code> on
    <code>html</code>), an entry and an exit become a short fade. With <code>--udx-motion: none</code> on the element or
    an ancestor, the end state applies at once. See <a href="/motion/accessibility">Reduced motion</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The element must accept a DOM reference: a component that does not forward its ref cannot be animated.</li>
    <li>A cancelled motion releases nothing, so the next motion carries from where it stopped.</li>
    <li>A value that is not neutral stays inline at the end of a motion (the <code>opacity</code> 0 of an exit), so a class that sets the same property is overridden until the binding is destroyed.</li>
  </ul>
</MotionPage>
